section('category-access','Credential access and gates by category','<p>All ten categories contain ten apps. Every count uses that full category denominator, including unknowns. Counts derive from saved access qualifications and their attached evidence, rather than potentially misleading gate labels. Developer-obtainable means a documented credential route; it does not certify free/trial access.</p>'+table(['Category','Access evidence','Developer-obtainable','Paid / entitlement','Admin / permissions','Review / API approval','Partner / outreach','Access unresolved','Evidence, onboarding unclassified'],ac)+'<p>Labels overlap: private credential creation can coexist with paid resources, role restrictions and public distribution review. Review counts include provider API-access approval; ordinary OAuth consent is counted under permissions. Partner/outreach can apply only to a named partner route or conditional support request. Zero means no qualifying saved evidence, not a proven absence of that gate.</p><p>CRM and productivity offer several documented credential routes, with permissions still relevant. Support and communications mix private prototypes with public-app review. Marketing and enrichment include approval or usage/plan gates; commerce needs installation and store entitlement checks. Developer platforms have credential routes but incomplete access coverage. Finance splits dashboard tokens from partner OAuth or commercial contracts. AI/transcript tools mix direct keys with Enterprise and usage-credit gates. Use the per-category unknown counts before generalizing.</p><details><summary>Classification boundaries and excluded onboarding claims</summary><p>'+esc(access_analysis['rules'])+'</p>'+table(['App ID','Why onboarding is not inferred'],[(i,esc(reason)) for i,reason in access_analysis['exclusions'].items()])+'</details>')
section('workflow','Research workflow and Composio usage','''<p>Assigned identities → bounded discovery and first-party retrieval → strict structured extraction → atomic evidence review → field-level evidence check → caveated dataset → targeted official-source corrections → fresh source review → targeted human documentation checks → reproducible pattern counts → standalone HTML.</p><p>The Python agent records source URLs, retrieval outcomes, source-text hashes, field references and failures. Semantic review distinguishes resource auth from token administration, API resources from product marketing, and native MCP from unrelated integrations. Checkpointed runs preserve the first pass and each correction layer.</p><p><strong>Composio was genuinely used in a separate augmentation path.</strong> The initial Composio authentication failed; the complete historical baseline used assigned seed-document discovery. After the key type was corrected to a Platform Project API key, tooling inspection selected <code>COMPOSIO_SEARCH_TAVILY</code>, version <code>20260903_00</code>, and eight successful augmentation searches were retained separately. It did not research all 100 apps through Composio. This final analysis/build makes no new research or paid API calls.</p><p><strong>Human boundary:</strong> Uday supplied 40 historical human review reports. Later targeted corrections and fresh current-source verification were conducted by a Codex agent, not fresh human app-detail verification. A separate completed targeted manual pass now records Uday’s documentation checks for 13 named apps. Codex transcribed the supplied evidence; this is not blinded independent review or executed integration testing.</p><details><summary>Historical run limits and correction history</summary><p>The semantic continuation froze at 96 extracted / 93 atom-reviewed / 84 valid field-reviewed apps after API credit exhaustion. Targeted official-source review added 117 field corrections across 14 apps; fresh review then added 25 corrections across 8 apps in a separate layer. Failed retrievals and unresolved identity never become negative product facts.</p></details>''')
vb='''<p><strong>No whole-dataset accuracy estimate is claimed.</strong> Evidence coverage, historical human review and fresh agent source review measure different things.</p>'''+table(['Layer','Denominator / result','Interpretation'],[['Historical first-pass human sample','7/35 scoreable claims correct before correction','Seed 42; 20 apps / 40 predeclared claims; 5 unclear and unscored.'],['Historical post-correction workflow','35/35 on the same scoreable claims','Judgments were supplied during correction; not independent accuracy. Historical sample agreement rose from 20% to 100% (+80 percentage points), not a population accuracy gain.'],['Fresh purposive source review','20 apps / 60 original records: 47 correct; 8 caveat_needed; 5 unsupported; 0 unverifiable','Six correct records are appropriately unresolved cases, not positive supported claims.'],['Bounded expansion','15 additional records: 3 correct; 12 caveat_needed','Separate from original 60; 25 field corrections across 8 apps applied after review. Corrected values are not counted as fresh independent successes.']])
vb+='<p>Fresh sources were observed before full sample-value comparison where feasible. Initial handoff/sample reading exposed saved values: the review was not fully blinded, statistically independent, or performed by a new human reviewer. It is a purposive risk sample and does not support a dataset-wide accuracy percentage.</p>'
